# Day 7 – Challenges

## Challenge 1 – Understanding Governor Limits

Initially, it was difficult to understand why correctly written code can still fail.

I learned that Salesforce limits the resources a transaction can consume.

## Challenge 2 – SOQL Inside Loops

I learned that a SOQL query inside a loop can execute repeatedly for every record.

This can cause the transaction to exceed the SOQL query limit.

## Challenge 3 – DML Inside Loops

DML inside a loop can consume too many DML statements.

The better approach is to collect records and perform DML together.

## Challenge 4 – Thinking in Collections

I learned to work with multiple records instead of designing only for a single record.

## Challenge 5 – Using Sets and Maps

I practised using:

- Set<Id> to collect unique Student IDs
- Map<Id, Student__c> to store queried Students
- List<Application__c> to process Applications

## Solution

I created a bulkified Apex class that:

1. Collects Student IDs.
2. Performs one SOQL query.
3. Stores Students in a Map.
4. Processes Applications using the Map.

## Key Improvement

I learned to ask:

"What happens when Salesforce sends 200 records?"

instead of only asking whether the code works for one record.
